'use strict';
// Desert County: 100 authored rescues in 10 connected chapters. One person per level, never repeated.
// Fields: n name · r role · t title · s situation · k what they know (tease) · l line once aboard ·
// x what they saw (revealed at home; finales use the chapter clue) · j/m Jun and Mara · h safety lesson key ·
// w companion (two-person pickup) · sup supplies to recover.
(globalThis.RescueStories=globalThis.RescueStories||[])[0]={chapters:[
{title:'The Last School Bus',arc:'Bus 14 broke down in the smoke at Old Pump. Its driver scattered the kids to safe spots and refused to leave until every one was found.',levels:[
{n:'Lucía',r:'student',t:'Forty-one cars',s:'Lucía, 11, carried her little brother’s inhaler back to the Old Pump canopy after Bus 14 stalled in the smoke. She has been counting cars ever since.',k:'saw who towed Bus 14 out of the fire line',l:'I counted forty-one cars. You’re the first one that stopped.',x:'Before the smoke closed in, a tow truck with one headlight hooked Bus 14 and dragged it clear of the fire line.',j:'Bus 14’s kids are scattered all over Old Pump.',m:'Then we find every single one of them.',h:'smoke'},
{n:'Mateo',r:'student',t:'The inhaler',s:'Mateo, 7, is sheltering with a neighbor behind a fallen power line, waiting for the inhaler his big sister Lucía carried away.',k:'remembers the song the tow driver was singing',l:'Is Lucía okay? She has my inhaler. She always has my inhaler.',x:'The tow-truck man was singing about a porch light. He waved at us like he knew us.',j:'A seven-year-old behind a downed cable.',m:'Then we go around it. Slowly.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Abebe',r:'student',t:'The high bank',s:'Abebe, 13, carried two first-graders to the high bank when the irrigation ditch overflowed. He hasn’t let go of their hands since.',k:'read something strange on the tow truck’s plate',l:'I didn’t let go once. Not once. Can I let go now?',x:'Someone had wiped the dust off just two letters on the tow truck’s plate: an M and a J.',j:'M and J… Mara, that’s us.',m:'Focus. Three kids on the high bank first.',h:'water'},
{n:'Priya',r:'student',t:'The wrong pickup',s:'Priya, 12, got a text from an unknown number telling her to walk to the quarry. She stayed at the Old Pump and asked Jun to check it.',k:'kept the strange text that mentions the garage',l:'Mom says: if it feels wrong, stay where they’ll look for you. So I stayed.',x:'The fake text said “the tow truck took your bus.” Whoever sent it knows about the garage’s truck.',j:'Someone is using the bus to lure kids away.',m:'Then nobody walks anywhere alone tonight.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Hiroshi',r:'student',t:'The bus tablet',s:'Hiroshi, 14, refused to give the bus tablet’s code to a man who said he was from the school board. He’s hiding it under his jacket.',k:'has the bus tablet’s last GPS log',l:'The code stays with me and Ms. Rosa. That’s the rule. I kept the rule.',x:'The tablet logged a second vehicle beside the bus at 6:12 a.m. Its ID matches the garage’s old tow truck.',j:'The garage’s truck, logged by a school bus?',m:'Dad sold that truck years ago. Didn’t he?',h:'code'},
{n:'Grace',r:'medic',t:'The nurse’s kit',s:'Grace, the school nurse, left a sealed allergy kit at the Old Pump before going back for stragglers. It has to reach the community garage.',k:'found a signature on the kit’s lid',l:'Every child on that list is breathing because someone carried this. Thank you.',x:'On the kit’s lid, in grease pencil: “Moved to safety. — T.” Dad signed every work order with a T.',j:'T. Tomás. That’s Dad’s signature.',m:'Lots of people sign with a T, Jun.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Sami',r:'student',t:'Biscuit',s:'Sami, 9, chased his runaway dog to the fenced pool behind the pump and stopped at the gate, just like his swim coach taught him. Biscuit came back to him.',k:'followed tow-chain tracks past the pool',l:'Biscuit found me. I found the gate. Then I just waited and sang.',x:'The tracks went past the pool and turned toward Dry Creek. A tow chain dragged a line through the dust.',j:'Tow-chain tracks, heading for Dry Creek.',m:'One rescue at a time. Sami first.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Daniela',r:'student',t:'The cracked footbridge',s:'Daniela, 15, could have crossed the cracked footbridge home in two minutes. She stayed on the safe side with a frightened sixth-grader instead.',k:'watched the tow truck stop at the footbridge',l:'Shortcut or not, I wasn’t going to let him be brave alone.',x:'The tow truck stopped at the footbridge too. The driver got out, shook his head, and drove the long way round.',j:'He took the long way. Like Dad always did.',m:'Like you always did, when you were scared.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Wren',r:'student',t:'The rusty dog',s:'Wren, 8, lost everyone in the smoke. Her phone died after one message: “by the big rusty dog.” Jun knows it’s the old dog-shaped sign behind the pump.',k:'drew the tow truck on her hand',l:'I knew someone would know the rusty dog. Everybody knows the rusty dog.',x:'She drew the tow truck on her hand in marker: one headlight, and a porch light painted on the door.',j:'Dad painted a porch light on our garage door.',m:'I remember. I helped him.',h:'landmark'},
{n:'Rosa',r:'school bus driver',t:'Fourteen for fourteen',s:'Rosa has driven Bus 14 for twenty-two years. She’s the last one at the Old Pump, holding the attendance list and refusing to leave until every name is checked.',k:'knows who really saved her bus',l:'Fourteen names. Fourteen checks. Now I can go home.',j:'Rosa’s still at the pump with her list.',m:'Then let’s make it fourteen for fourteen.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'Eighty Candles',arc:'Josefa’s family drove in from four states for her eightieth birthday at the Dry Creek campground. Then the creek flash-flooded.',levels:[
{n:'Omar',r:'baker',t:'The birthday cake',s:'Omar baked his grandmother Josefa’s eightieth birthday cake and left it on a dry boulder when the creek rose. The cake still has to reach her.',k:'saw who pulled their cars out of the wash',l:'Eighty candles. I wasn’t letting the creek have them.',x:'At dawn a tow truck pulled our cars out of the wash, one by one. The driver wouldn’t take any money.',j:'We’re rescuing… a cake?',m:'We’re rescuing a birthday. Get in.',h:'water',sup:1},
{n:'Leila',r:'medic',t:'Shade',s:'Leila, a nurse, is keeping her elderly uncle cool in the only shade at Dry Creek. It’s 44 degrees and the camp’s water ran out an hour ago.',k:'heard the tow driver ask about two kids',l:'I kept telling Uncle Farid, “one more minute.” I ran out of minutes. Then you came.',x:'The tow driver asked if anyone had seen two grown-up kids named Mara and Jun. He looked so tired.',j:'He was asking about us, Mara.',m:'Then why didn’t he just come home?',h:'heat'},
{n:'Farid',r:'mechanic',t:'Same hands',s:'Farid, 76, a retired mechanic, is stranded at his car on the camp road. A downed power line has the road blocked on both sides.',k:'recognized the tow driver’s hands',l:'Forty years fixing engines, and I can’t fix getting old. Thank you for coming.',x:'He fixed my radiator without a word. Same hands as the garage mechanic I knew thirty years ago.',j:'Thirty years ago, the garage had one mechanic.',m:'Dad. You think it’s Dad.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Yasmin',r:'student',w:'Zaid',t:'Cousins',s:'Yasmin, 10, and her cousin Zaid, 9, pulled off the trail when the dust storm hit and waited exactly where their parents told them to.',k:'saw a tow truck signal through the dust',l:'Zaid said we were lost. I said no, we were waiting. There’s a difference.',x:'In the dust storm a tow truck stopped beside us, flashed its light three times, and waited until it passed.',j:'Three flashes. Dad’s “I’m okay” signal.',m:'Stop. Don’t hope yet.',h:'dust'},
{n:'Nadira',r:'radio volunteer',t:'Not while I’m breathing',s:'Nadira runs the campground radio. A stranger’s message is telling families the evacuation road is closed and to drive to a private lot instead.',k:'heard the tow driver answer on the channel',l:'I almost believed it. Then a voice I trusted said “don’t.”',x:'The fake message said the garage had been sold. The tow driver came on the channel and said: “Not while I’m breathing.”',j:'The garage was never sold.',m:'Someone wants people to think it was.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Rafi',r:'lifeguard trainee',t:'The rope by the gate',s:'Rafi, 17, stayed outside the locked campground pool after the flood filled it, keeping two kids back from the edge until help came.',k:'found something the tow driver left at the gate',l:'Everyone wanted to jump in. Somebody had to be the boring one.',x:'He left a coil of rope by the pool gate and drove off. Rope with the garage’s red tag.',j:'Red tags. We still have a box of those.',m:'Then someone has the other box.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Dalia',r:'photographer',t:'The washout',s:'Dalia was photographing the reunion when the low-water crossing washed out. She chose to wait on the high side instead of driving through.',k:'photographed the tow truck’s door',l:'I take pictures of people I love. I wanted to be around to take more.',x:'I photographed the tow truck at the washout. Look at the door: “Keep the lights on.”',j:'Keep the lights on. That was Dad’s—',m:'I know what it was.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Yusuf',r:'shepherd',t:'The split cactus',s:'Yusuf drove his goats to safety and lost his phone signal. His last message said he’d wait at the split saguaro on the ridge.',k:'gave the tow driver directions',l:'The goats are safe. I didn’t think anyone would come for the goatherd.',x:'He asked me which way the county garage was. Like he’d been away a long time.',j:'Dad never needed directions home.',m:'Maybe he forgot the way. Maybe on purpose.',h:'landmark'},
{n:'Samira',r:'pharmacist',t:'Josefa’s medicine',s:'Samira, the family’s pharmacist, packed Josefa’s heart medication in a sealed cooler before the flood. The cooler is on the camp table, waiting.',k:'found a note taped inside the cooler',l:'Eighty years old and she still won’t take her pills unless I’m watching.',x:'Inside the cooler’s lid: “For Josefa. From the garage, like always. — T.”',j:'“Like always.” Dad knew her.',m:'Dad knew everybody. That was his whole problem.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Josefa',r:'grandmother',t:'Eighty years',s:'Josefa, 80, is sitting in her folding chair on the last dry rise of the campground, wearing her birthday crown, waiting for her family to be together again.',k:'remembers the night someone left a thermos',l:'Eighty years, and my whole family is coming home on the same night. That’s the gift.',j:'Last one at Dry Creek.',m:'And it’s her birthday. Let’s not be late.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'The Night Shift',arc:'KDCR, the county’s tiny volunteer radio station on Radio Hill, kept broadcasting evacuation news all night. Its crew is still up there.',levels:[
{n:'Benedict',r:'radio volunteer',t:'Porch Light Blues',s:'Benedict, the night DJ, kept talking through the smoke so listeners wouldn’t feel alone. The studio is filling with smoke and he finally signed off.',k:'knows who keeps requesting the same song',l:'Four hours talking to strangers so nobody felt alone. Who was talking to me? You were.',x:'Every night at 2 a.m. someone requests “Porch Light Blues.” Always from a tow-truck radio.',j:'That was Dad’s song. Our song.',m:'Thousands of people know that song, Jun.',h:'smoke'},
{n:'Esperanza',r:'sound engineer',t:'The guy-wire',s:'Esperanza patched the transmitter three times tonight. Now a snapped antenna guy-wire is lying across the only path down the hill.',k:'traced the tow truck’s radio signal',l:'I can fix any signal. I can’t fix a hill.',x:'The 2 a.m. requests come from a mobile radio tuned to the garage’s old frequency.',j:'The garage frequency? Nobody uses that.',m:'Somebody does.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Tobias',r:'student intern',t:'First night on air',s:'Tobias, 16, stayed for his first overnight shift. Now runoff has flooded the road at the base of Radio Hill.',k:'recorded the tow driver’s voice',l:'Best first night ever. Worst first night ever. Both.',x:'I recorded the 2 a.m. caller. He says “for my kids, wherever they are” before every song.',j:'Play it. Please.',m:'Later. When we’re home.',h:'water'},
{n:'Halima',r:'dispatcher',t:'The false call sign',s:'Halima noticed a caller using the sheriff’s call sign with the wrong code. She refused to relay his pickup orders until Jun confirmed them.',k:'knows who the fake caller was looking for',l:'Wrong code, wrong voice. I wasn’t sending anyone anywhere on that.',x:'The fake caller asked where the garage’s tow truck parks at night. I didn’t tell him.',j:'Someone is hunting for Dad’s truck.',m:'Then we find it first.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Viktor',r:'transmitter engineer',t:'The station password',s:'Viktor refused to give the station’s broadcast password to a man who wanted to air a “company announcement.” He locked himself in the transmitter shed.',k:'heard what the company announcement said',l:'Forty years on this hill. Nobody puts lies on my transmitter.',x:'The announcement claimed the old garage caused the blackouts. They wanted it on air before dawn.',j:'They’re blaming the garage. Blaming Dad.',m:'Then the truth goes on air first.',h:'code'},
{n:'Chen',r:'medic',t:'Insulin run',s:'Chen, a volunteer medic, left a sealed insulin cooler at the station for listeners who called in. It needs to reach the clinic tent at the garage.',k:'found the tow driver’s handwriting on a listener list',l:'Twelve people on that list. Twelve people sleeping safe tonight.',x:'Next to three names on the list: “Delivered. — T.” He’s been doing this for weeks.',j:'Weeks. He’s been out here for weeks.',m:'And never once came home.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Ruby',r:'listener',t:'Caller number nine',s:'Ruby, 10, called the station to say she was waiting outside the reservoir gate with her grandpa, safe side, like the DJ said.',k:'talked to the 2 a.m. caller on air',l:'The DJ said stay on the safe side. So we did. Did I do good?',x:'The 2 a.m. man told me on air: “If you see my kids, tell them the light’s still on.”',j:'Ruby. What else did he say?',m:'Jun. Get her home first.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Ezra',r:'tower climber',t:'The ridge road',s:'Ezra climbed down from the antenna mast to find the ridge road cracked open. He decided to wait for a ride instead of risking it.',k:'saw the tow truck’s route from the mast',l:'I’ve climbed three hundred feet in the wind. Waiting was the hard part.',x:'From the mast I saw the tow truck parked on every road out of the county, one after another, all night.',j:'He’s been guarding the roads.',m:'For whom?',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Ana',r:'listener',t:'Under the twin antennas',s:'Ana, 70, walked up Radio Hill to thank the night crew and lost her way in the dark. She’s waiting under the twin antennas, the only landmark she can see.',k:'knows the tow driver’s name',l:'I just wanted to say thank you to them. Now I get to say it to you too.',x:'I asked the tow driver his name. He said, “Just tell them it was the porch-light man.”',j:'The porch-light man.',m:'Dad hated being thanked.',h:'night'},
{n:'Adaeze',r:'station manager',t:'Sign-off',s:'Adaeze, KDCR’s station manager, is the last one on Radio Hill. She stayed to log the final evacuation report before shutting the transmitter down.',k:'kept the station’s call log',l:'We stayed on air until everyone was off the road. Now I can go off air too.',j:'One more on Radio Hill.',m:'And the call log.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'Ten Coffees',arc:'The County Diner never closes. Tonight its staff and regulars are scattered across the highway, and the coffee is still on.',levels:[
{n:'Flo',r:'waitress',t:'Two cups',s:'Flo has worked the diner’s night shift for thirty years. A grass fire is smoking across the parking lot and she walked the regulars out before herself.',k:'knows what the tow driver orders every night',l:'Thirty years pouring coffee. First time anyone came to pour me out of here.',x:'The tow man always orders two coffees and leaves one untouched, for somebody who isn’t there.',j:'Two coffees. One for Mom.',m:'Mom’s been gone eight years, Jun.',h:'smoke'},
{n:'Marcus',r:'line cook',t:'The kitchen door',s:'Marcus, the line cook, is stuck behind the diner where a power line came down across the delivery lane.',k:'cooked the tow driver’s order every night',l:'I made four hundred breakfasts this week. Nobody ever made me one.',x:'Eggs over easy, burnt toast, and he asks for the toast burnt. Every night.',j:'Burnt toast. Dad always burned the toast.',m:'He said it tasted like home.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Pete',r:'trucker',t:'Rig in the storm',s:'Pete, 63, pulled his rig fully off the highway when the dust storm hit, lights off, and waited like he taught every young trucker to.',k:'helped the tow truck pull out a car',l:'Forty years of trucking. First time I needed a tow, it came with a friend.',x:'I helped the tow man pull a family’s car out of the ditch. He wouldn’t let me tell anybody.',j:'He keeps hiding.',m:'Maybe he thinks we don’t want to see him.',h:'dust'},
{n:'Ivy',r:'dishwasher',t:'Changed pickup',s:'Ivy, 19, got a text from her “manager” telling her to meet him at an empty lot. She stayed at the diner and called Flo to check.',k:'saw who the fake message came from',l:'Flo taught me: if it’s not her voice, it’s not my shift.',x:'The fake text came from the same company that wants the garage’s land.',j:'The company again.',m:'They want the land. They need Dad gone.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Ramón',r:'diner owner',t:'The cash box',s:'Ramón owns the diner. A man offered to “protect” the charity jar if Ramón gave him the alarm code. Ramón refused and locked up.',k:'knows who has been paying everyone’s tab',l:'That jar feeds twelve families. Nobody gets the code for that.',x:'Somebody has been paying for stranded drivers’ meals for a month. The receipts say “T.”',j:'Dad’s paying people’s breakfasts.',m:'With what money?',h:'code'},
{n:'Beth',r:'pie baker',t:'The sealed box',s:'Beth, the pie baker, kept the diner’s sealed EpiPen box safe for the allergy kids who eat there after school. It must get to the clinic.',k:'found a note under the pie counter',l:'Every kid who eats my pie deserves to be safe eating it.',x:'Under the counter, in his handwriting: “One coffee for every driver who makes it home tonight. Put it on my tab.”',j:'He’s counting everyone he gets home.',m:'Like Rosa and her list.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Kofi',r:'regular',t:'The stock pond',s:'Kofi, a diner regular, stayed outside the fenced stock pond behind the diner while a calf struggled in the water, and called a rancher instead of going in.',k:'saw the tow truck at the pond',l:'Everyone wants to be a hero. Sometimes the hero move is making the call.',x:'The tow man arrived with the rancher, waded in on a rope line, and was gone before anyone said thanks.',j:'Dad always said: the right person, the right gear.',m:'He said it about everything.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Linh',r:'delivery driver',t:'The washed road',s:'Linh delivers to the diner every morning. The county road is washed out, and she parked to wait instead of driving across.',k:'delivered something to the tow driver',l:'Four years delivering. First time I got to be the package.',x:'I delivered a parcel to the tow man: two old house keys on a ring shaped like a porch light.',j:'Our house keys. He kept our house keys.',m:'He kept everything.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Hank',r:'retired sheriff',t:'Night watch',s:'Hank, the retired sheriff, stayed with his car on the highway shoulder at night after it died, hazards on, waiting like he told drivers for forty years.',k:'knows why the tow driver left the garage',l:'I told folks to wait for help for forty years. Felt strange taking my own advice.',x:'He told me he left the garage because he couldn’t face it empty. He said he’d come back when it was ready.',j:'Ready for what?',m:'For us. Ready for us.',h:'night'},
{n:'Dolores',r:'diner founder',t:'Last cup',s:'Dolores opened the County Diner in 1979. She’s still at the counter with a fresh pot on, waiting for the last of her people to be safe.',k:'kept the tow driver’s tab for years',l:'The coffee stays on until everyone’s home. That was always the rule.',j:'Last one at the diner.',m:'Last one at the diner. And the tab.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'The Mural',arc:'Last spring, the kids of Desert County painted a mural on the old Water Tower. Tonight the people who made it are stranded around it.',levels:[
{n:'Amara',r:'muralist',t:'The porch light at the top',s:'Amara designed the Water Tower mural with fourteen kids. She’s waiting at its base in the heat, out of water, after guiding the painters down.',k:'painted something the tow driver asked for',l:'We painted hope on a water tower. Felt silly. Doesn’t feel silly tonight.',x:'The tow man asked us to paint a porch light at the very top of the tower. He paid for the paint.',j:'A porch light you can see from the whole county.',m:'So someone could always find their way home.',h:'heat'},
{n:'Caleb',r:'utility worker',t:'The tower line',s:'Caleb was checking the tower’s pump when a power line sparked and fell across the service road.',k:'saw the tow driver repairing the tower light',l:'I keep the water running for six thousand people. Tonight I just needed one ride.',x:'He climbed the tower every month to change the bulb in the painted porch light. Never missed once.',j:'Every month. For a painted light.',m:'It wasn’t for the light.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Sofía',r:'student painter',t:'Blue handprints',s:'Sofía, 12, left her blue handprint on the mural. The tower’s overflow has flooded the road home, so she’s waiting on the dry steps.',k:'saw where the tow truck parks at night',l:'My handprint is on that tower forever. I wanted me to be around forever too.',x:'The tow truck sleeps under the tower some nights. He leaves the cab light on.',j:'Under the tower. We were so close.',m:'We were never looking.',h:'water'},
{n:'Ibrahim',r:'pump operator',t:'The fake shutdown',s:'Ibrahim got a radio order to shut off the town’s water. The voice didn’t know the plant’s code word, so he refused and waited for Jun to confirm.',k:'heard the tow driver warn the plant',l:'Six thousand people drink that water. A stranger’s voice wasn’t enough.',x:'An hour before the fake order, the tow man called: “Don’t trust any shutdown tonight. Not without the word.”',j:'Dad knew it was coming.',m:'Dad knows something big.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Kaya',r:'student',t:'The mural password',s:'Kaya, 15, runs the mural’s online donation page. Someone posing as the county asked for her password “to verify funds.” She logged out instead.',k:'found an old donation in the records',l:'That page raised money for the fire crews. No way I’m handing it over.',x:'The very first donation was anonymous. The note said: “For the porch light. Keep it lit.”',j:'Dad funded the mural.',m:'Dad funded the porch light.',h:'code'},
{n:'Meera',r:'veterinarian',t:'Ranch vaccines',s:'Meera, the county vet, left sealed animal vaccines at the tower for the ranches cut off by the fires. They need to reach the garage’s supply room.',k:'treated the tow driver’s dog',l:'Seventy ranch dogs will sleep safe because of that box.',x:'The tow man brings an old dog to my clinic. Grey muzzle. He calls her Lucky.',j:'Lucky? Lucky’s still alive?',m:'She was a puppy when you left for college.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Bastian',r:'lifeguard',t:'The reservoir fence',s:'Bastian kept a crowd of teenagers back from the overflowing reservoir fence until the flood crew arrived.',k:'watched the tow driver at the reservoir',l:'Nobody thanks the guy who says “get back.” You did, though.',x:'The tow man stood at the fence for an hour, just looking at the porch light on the tower.',j:'He was looking at home.',m:'From the outside.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Selin',r:'surveyor',t:'Rockfall',s:'Selin was surveying the tower hill when part of the cut collapsed onto the road. She backed away from the loose slope to wait.',k:'mapped the tow truck’s daily route',l:'I measure land for a living. I had no idea how far it was to safety.',x:'His route every day: the tower, the diner, the creek, the bus stop. Every place our family used to go.',j:'He’s visiting us. Every place but home.',m:'Because home was empty.',h:'rockfall'},
{n:'Gus',r:'ladder inspector',t:'The chalk ladder',s:'Gus, 81, inspects the tower ladder every year. He’s sitting under the mural’s sun, his description of where to find him exactly right.',k:'saw chalk marks on the tower ladder',l:'Eighty-one years old and still climbing. Not tonight, though.',x:'He told me: “When my kids come looking, the ladder will show them which way I went.”',j:'He wants us to find him.',m:'Then he should have called.',h:'landmark'},
{n:'Ndidi',r:'water keeper',t:'The tower keeper',s:'Ndidi has kept the Water Tower running for thirty years. She’s the last one there, making sure the overflow is safe before she leaves.',k:'knows what the arrows on the ladder mean',l:'I kept the water clean for thirty years. Tonight someone kept me safe.',j:'Last one at the tower.',m:'And the arrows.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'The Stalled 4:10',arc:'The 4:10 passenger train stalled at the Rail Crossing when the line lost power. Its passengers and crew spread out to safe ground along the tracks.',levels:[
{n:'Oksana',r:'passenger',t:'Brake smoke',s:'Oksana, a violinist on her way to her first concert, walked away from the 4:10 when its brakes began to smoke. She’s waiting upwind with her violin case.',k:'saw the tow truck racing beside the train',l:'I was going to play for three hundred people tonight. I’d rather play for you.',x:'A tow truck raced the train along the frontage road, flashing its light, warning the driver to stop before the crossing.',j:'He warned the train.',m:'He warns everybody but us.',h:'smoke'},
{n:'Dev',r:'track worker',t:'The fallen line',s:'Dev, a track worker, is cut off between two sections of fallen overhead line. He’s staying put, exactly as his safety training says.',k:'knows who cut power to the line in time',l:'Twenty years on the rails. Never wanted to see a train so badly.',x:'Someone called the rail office and had the power cut seconds before the line fell. The caller said, “Reyes Garage.”',j:'Reyes Garage. He used our name.',m:'He never stopped using it.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Lupe',r:'grandmother',t:'Knitting in the heat',s:'Lupe, 84, is knitting a blanket for her great-grandson in the only shade by the tracks. She’s been rationing one bottle of water for two hours.',k:'met the tow driver on the platform',l:'I’ve knitted through three wars and one divorce. A little heat won’t stop me. But thank you.',x:'He gave me his water and told me his daughter used to knit. Badly, he said, laughing.',j:'You were terrible at knitting.',m:'He kept the scarf I made him. I bet he kept it.',h:'heat'},
{n:'Arjun',r:'student traveler',t:'The changed platform',s:'Arjun, 19, got a message telling all passengers to walk two miles to a “company shuttle.” He stayed by the train and asked the conductor first.',k:'found out who was running the fake shuttle',l:'Two miles in the dark to a shuttle nobody’d heard of? No thanks.',x:'The shuttle belonged to the company that wants the garage’s land. They were collecting witnesses.',j:'Witnesses to what?',m:'To whatever story they’re telling about Dad.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Celeste',r:'ticket clerk',t:'The manifest',s:'Celeste, the ticket clerk, refused to unlock the passenger manifest for a man who said he was “security.” She kept the tablet and walked to the safe zone.',k:'saw a name on the manifest',l:'Those are people’s names. They’re not for sale.',x:'One name on the manifest was booked every week for a year and never boarded: “T. Reyes, plus two.”',j:'Plus two. Us.',m:'He kept buying us tickets.',h:'code'},
{n:'Joaquín',r:'medic',t:'The sealed bag',s:'Joaquín, a paramedic riding home off-duty, packed the train’s sealed trauma bag at the crossing for the clinic crew. It still needs carrying.',k:'treated the tow driver’s burned hands',l:'I patched him up and he went straight back out. Some people don’t know how to stop.',x:'His hands were burned from pulling a kid out of a car. He just said: “Better mine than hers.”',j:'Dad’s hurt?',m:'Dad’s always hurt. He just never says.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Pip',r:'student',t:'The duck pond',s:'Pip, 6, saw ducks struggling in the flooded pond behind the crossing and stayed behind the fence, shouting for a grown-up, like her mom taught her.',k:'got a gift from the tow driver',l:'The ducks were scared. I was scared. But I didn’t climb. I promise.',x:'The tow man gave me a sticker of a porch light. He said his kids used to collect them.',j:'We had a whole book of those stickers.',m:'You cried when the book got wet.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Nabil',r:'signalman',t:'Signal box',s:'Nabil, the signalman, stayed in his box until the last train was stopped, then found both access roads cracked. He’s waiting for a safe way out.',k:'found something left in the signal box',l:'My job is red, yellow, green. Tonight I just wanted someone to say go.',x:'Somebody left a work jacket folded on my chair. The patch on the chest says “Keep the lights on.”',j:'Dad’s jacket.',m:'He was here. Right here.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Zuri',r:'musician',t:'The crossing bell',s:'Zuri, a drummer, got off the stalled train at night with dead phone and drums. She’s sitting by the crossing bell, the loudest thing she could find.',k:'heard the tow driver singing',l:'I banged that bell like a drum for an hour. Best gig of my life, honestly.',x:'Someone was singing “Porch Light Blues” in the dark, walking along the tracks. Badly. Beautifully.',j:'He always sang it off-key.',m:'On purpose. To make us laugh.',h:'night'},
{n:'Ellis',r:'conductor',t:'All aboard',s:'Ellis, the 4:10’s conductor, walked the whole stalled train twice to make sure no one was left. He’s the last one at the Rail Crossing.',k:'found what was left in the signal box',l:'Every passenger accounted for. Now the conductor can go home.',j:'Last one at the crossing.',m:'And whatever’s in the signal box.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'Market Day',arc:'Saturday is market day at the old Dust Market. The vendors stayed to pack up when the storm warnings came, and now they’re stranded.',levels:[
{n:'Carmen',r:'fruit seller',t:'Mangoes in the storm',s:'Carmen has sold fruit at the Dust Market for fifty years. When the dust storm rolled in, she covered her stall, sat in her truck with the lights off, and waited.',k:'gave the tow driver mangoes every week',l:'Fifty years, and the dust has never beaten me. But I’ll take the ride.',x:'Every Saturday he buys two mangoes. One for him, one he leaves on the dashboard.',j:'Mom loved mangoes.',m:'He still buys hers.',h:'dust'},
{n:'Bilal',r:'tailor',t:'The awning wire',s:'Bilal, the market tailor, is stuck behind his stall where a lighting wire tore loose in the wind. He hasn’t touched it.',k:'mended the tow driver’s jacket',l:'I sew things back together for a living. Tonight I needed sewing back together.',x:'I patched his jacket last month. He asked me to stitch two names inside the collar: Mara, Jun.',j:'Our names. In his collar.',m:'Close to his heart. Of course.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Maya',r:'student',t:'The lemonade stand',s:'Maya, 9, ran a lemonade stand at the market. When the heat soared she moved into the shade of the arch and gave away her last cup.',k:'sold lemonade to the tow driver',l:'I gave my last lemonade to a grandma. So I didn’t have one. That’s okay.',x:'He paid me ten dollars for one lemonade and said, “My daughter ran a stand like this. She overcharged too.”',j:'You did overcharge.',m:'It was a business.',h:'heat'},
{n:'Joseph',r:'accordionist',t:'The blind musician',s:'Joseph, a blind accordionist, received a message that his niece would collect him at the east gate. He knows her voice, and it wasn’t hers. He stayed.',k:'recognized the tow driver by voice',l:'I don’t need eyes to know a lie. I need ears and a little patience.',x:'I know every voice in this market. The tow man’s voice is the same as the garage man who fixed my van in 2009.',j:'Dad fixed his van.',m:'Dad fixed everyone’s van.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Fatima',r:'spice merchant',t:'The lockbox',s:'Fatima, the spice merchant, refused to give the market’s lockbox code to “inspectors” who arrived during the storm. She hid the box in a spice crate.',k:'knows what the inspectors were after',l:'Saffron, cumin, and the market’s savings. All safe.',x:'They were after the market’s petition to save the old garage. Four hundred signatures.',j:'A petition. People want the garage back.',m:'People want him back.',h:'code'},
{n:'Beatriz',r:'herbalist',t:'The sealed tea',s:'Beatriz packed the market clinic’s sealed medicine chest when the storm came. It needs to reach the garage’s clinic tent.',k:'found a list in the medicine chest',l:'Medicines for forty families. I wasn’t leaving them behind.',x:'Taped inside the chest: a list of families he’s driven to the clinic. Ours is first, crossed out, with “someday” written beside it.',j:'Someday.',m:'Today.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Diego',r:'student',t:'Goats and the trough',s:'Diego, 11, tends goats at the market. When the flooded trough pen overflowed he stayed outside the fence and whistled the goats to him.',k:'saw where the tow truck goes after market',l:'The goats came when I whistled. I knew they would.',x:'After market he drives into the canyon. Every single Saturday.',j:'The canyon. Canyon Gate.',m:'Then that’s where he’s been living.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Kwame',r:'furniture maker',t:'The bench',s:'Kwame, a furniture maker, chose to wait beside his truck rather than cross the flooded dip in the market road.',k:'built something for the tow driver',l:'I build chairs. I finally got to sit in somebody else’s.',x:'He paid me to build a workbench. Big, old-style. He said it was for a garage that would open again.',j:'A workbench. For our garage.',m:'He’s been rebuilding it. Piece by piece.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Lila',r:'fortune teller',t:'The painted tent',s:'Lila, the market’s fortune teller, lost her phone in the dust. Her last message: “by the purple tent with the moon.” Jun knows it.',k:'read the tow driver’s cards',l:'I tell people what’s coming. I never saw you coming. Lovely surprise.',x:'I read his cards once. He laughed and said, “Just tell me if my kids are coming home.” I said yes.',j:'You told him yes.',m:'Then let’s make you right.',h:'landmark'},
{n:'Emmanuel',r:'market master',t:'Closing bell',s:'Emmanuel has run the Dust Market for twenty years. He stayed to ring the closing bell and make sure every vendor got out.',k:'knows what the traders saw in the canyon',l:'The bell rang, everyone went home. That was always the deal.',j:'Last one at the market.',m:'And what the traders saw.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'The Ones Who Waited',arc:'West Bridge cracked in the floods. On the far side, the people who chose to wait instead of risking it are still waiting.',levels:[
{n:'Aiyana',r:'expectant mother',w:'Jonah',t:'Due next week',s:'Aiyana, eight months pregnant, and her husband Jonah left their car when smoke drifted across the bridge road. They’re waiting upwind on the shoulder.',k:'saw the tow driver turn back for them',l:'We were going to name her after whoever saved us. I hope you like your name.',x:'A tow truck drove past, stopped, reversed, and waited with us until the smoke cleared. Then he left before we could ask his name.',j:'He turns back. He always turns back.',m:'He taught us that.',h:'smoke'},
{n:'Gideon',r:'dog rescuer',t:'Six dogs',s:'Gideon runs a dog rescue. He’s stuck behind a fallen line with six frightened dogs in his van and isn’t going near it.',k:'met the tow driver’s old dog',l:'Six dogs, zero panic. Okay, some panic. Mostly mine.',x:'An old grey dog rode in the tow truck. She howled at every porch light we passed.',j:'Lucky. That’s Lucky.',m:'She always howled at porch lights.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Hope',r:'foster mom',t:'Four kids, one van',s:'Hope, a foster mom, pulled her van onto dry ground when the creek rose over the bridge approach. Four kids are singing in the back.',k:'heard the tow driver talk about his kids',l:'Four kids, one van, eleven songs. We were fine. We were really fine.',x:'He told my kids: “Never stop looking for your people. And never stop letting them find you.”',j:'Letting them find you. He’s been waiting for us to find him.',m:'We should have looked sooner.',h:'water'},
{n:'Malik',r:'student',t:'The bridge rumor',s:'Malik, 16, saw a viral post saying the bridge was safe and a convoy was crossing. He checked the county channel instead, and it wasn’t safe.',k:'traced who posted the bridge rumor',l:'Everyone in my group chat was going. I was the only one who checked.',x:'The fake “bridge is safe” post came from the company account. They wanted traffic on the bridge, and someone else blamed.',j:'They’d have blamed Dad.',m:'They’re blaming him for everything.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Odette',r:'bank teller',t:'The vault code',s:'Odette, a bank teller, was asked by phone to read out the branch vault code “for an emergency transfer.” She hung up and walked to the bridge shelter.',k:'knows who has been paying the garage’s taxes',l:'Thirty years at the bank. Never once gave out a code. Not starting tonight.',x:'Someone has paid the garage’s property tax every year in cash, so the company couldn’t take it. Signed: T.',j:'He saved the garage. Every year.',m:'For us. Every year.',h:'code'},
{n:'Rashid',r:'pharmacist',t:'Across the river',s:'Rashid, the riverside pharmacist, packed a sealed box of prescriptions for the families on the far side. It’s waiting at the bridge shelter.',k:'filled a prescription for the tow driver',l:'People need medicine on both sides of a river.',x:'I filled his blood-pressure pills. He laughed and said his daughter would yell at him for skipping them.',j:'You would.',m:'I will.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Clara',r:'swim coach',t:'The river steps',s:'Clara, a swim coach, kept a crowd of teenagers away from the flooded river steps until the water crew arrived.',k:'saw the tow driver at the river',l:'I teach kids to swim. Tonight I taught them to wait.',x:'He stood on the far bank with a rope and the water crew, ready, but he let the professionals go in first.',j:'Right person, right gear.',m:'He learned his own lesson.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Nico',r:'cyclist',t:'The cliff road',s:'Nico was cycling home when rocks slid across the cliff road. He backed away from the loose slope and is waiting at the bend.',k:'saw the tow truck clear the rocks',l:'Ninety kilometers on a bike. I thought the hardest part was the hills.',x:'At dawn the tow truck pushed the rocks off the road so the ambulance could pass. Then it drove off toward the canyon.',j:'Toward Canyon Gate again.',m:'He lives out there.',h:'rockfall'},
{n:'Freddie',r:'retired pilot',t:'Runway lights',s:'Freddie, a retired pilot, is stranded at night by the closed bridge, sitting on his car hood with a flashlight, waving like a runway marshal.',k:'saw the tow driver’s camp from the air',l:'Forty years landing planes. Turns out I’m the one who needed guiding in.',x:'From my plane last week I saw a camp beyond Canyon Gate: a tow truck, a tent, and a string of porch lights.',j:'Porch lights. In the desert.',m:'He made himself a home.',h:'night'},
{n:'Marguerite',r:'bridge keeper',t:'The last to cross',s:'Marguerite has kept West Bridge for thirty-five years. She closed it herself tonight and stayed until everyone on her side was safe.',k:'knows why the tire tracks stop at the bridge',l:'I closed my own bridge tonight. Hardest thing I’ve ever done. Right thing, though.',j:'Last one at West Bridge.',m:'And the tracks.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'The Last Round',arc:'Canyon Gate’s rangers make one last round before the canyon closes for the night. Tonight that round didn’t finish.',levels:[
{n:'Tallulah',r:'park ranger',t:'Heat on the trail',s:'Tallulah, a ranger, has been bringing water to stranded hikers all day. Now she’s the one out of water, resting in the shade of the gate house.',k:'checks in with the tow driver every night',l:'I bring people water for a living. Weird to be the one who needs it.',x:'He checks in at the gate every night at nine. Always asks: “Anyone come looking for me?”',j:'Every night at nine.',m:'We never came.',h:'heat'},
{n:'Bodhi',r:'hiker',t:'Slide',s:'Bodhi, a hiker, stopped short when the trail ahead collapsed in a rockfall. He backed away and is waiting at the trail fork.',k:'saw the tow driver’s camp up close',l:'I came out here to find myself. Found a rockslide instead.',x:'His camp has a workbench, a radio, and a photo of a garage with two kids on the roof.',j:'That’s us. On the garage roof.',m:'Dad took that picture.',h:'rockfall'},
{n:'Yvette',r:'scout leader',w:'Leo',t:'Troop 9',s:'Yvette, a scout leader, and her scout Leo stayed on high ground while flash water ran through the canyon wash below.',k:'earned a badge from the tow driver',l:'Leo’s getting his patience badge tonight. So am I.',x:'The tow man taught my troop how to tie a tow knot. He said, “My son never learned it. Too busy reading.”',j:'I was reading.',m:'You were always reading.',h:'water'},
{n:'Aurelio',r:'photographer',t:'Starlight',s:'Aurelio was shooting the stars when a radio message told everyone to drive to a “company rescue point.” He checked with the rangers first.',k:'photographed the tow truck’s camp at night',l:'I came for the stars. Ended up with the best shot of my life anyway.',x:'My long exposure: his camp at midnight, every porch light on, and him sitting alone facing the road home.',j:'Facing home. Every night.',m:'Waiting for us.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Ximena',r:'geologist',t:'Core samples',s:'Ximena, a geologist, refused to give the research station’s door code to men claiming to be from the company. She locked up and walked to the gate.',k:'found out what the company wants',l:'Rocks don’t lie. People do. I prefer rocks.',x:'The company wants the garage’s land for a pipeline. The old garage is the last property standing in the way.',j:'So that’s why.',m:'Dad stood in their way. Alone.',h:'code'},
{n:'Ashwin',r:'doctor',t:'The canyon clinic',s:'Ashwin, the canyon’s volunteer doctor, sealed the clinic’s supply crate for the evacuation. It needs to reach the garage tonight.',k:'treated the tow driver last week',l:'I patch up the whole canyon. Tonight the canyon patched me.',x:'He came in exhausted. I told him to rest. He said he’d rest when the garage was full again.',j:'Full again.',m:'With us in it.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Pilar',r:'climbing instructor',t:'The plunge pool',s:'Pilar kept her climbing class back from the flooded plunge pool at the canyon mouth, behind the safety rail, until the water dropped.',k:'climbed with the tow driver once',l:'I teach people to trust a rope. Tonight I trusted a stranger.',x:'He climbed the gate rock once to string a porch light at the top. Said someone should always be able to see it.',j:'Another porch light.',m:'He’s lighting a path.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Wes',r:'trail builder',t:'The washed bridge',s:'Wes, a trail builder, found his footbridge washed out and chose to wait instead of wading across.',k:'built the tow driver a road',l:'I build trails so people get home. Tonight I needed one.',x:'He paid me to clear an old track from the canyon to the Last Outpost. Said he’d need it soon.',j:'The Last Outpost.',m:'That’s where this ends.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Opal',r:'astronomer',t:'Under Orion',s:'Opal, an astronomer, got stranded at night when her jeep died. She’s waiting by the telescope dome, its red light on, the only light for miles.',k:'saw the tow driver’s signal from the dome',l:'I look at lights millions of years old. Yours was the best one tonight.',x:'From the dome I saw his headlight blink three times toward the county. Every night. Toward your garage.',j:'Three blinks. I’m okay.',m:'Every night. For years.',h:'night'},
{n:'Kenji',r:'chief ranger',t:'Closing the gate',s:'Kenji, the chief ranger, is closing Canyon Gate for the night. He won’t leave until his whole team is accounted for.',k:'knows what the headlight beyond the gate means',l:'Gate’s closed. Everyone’s out. Thirty years, and that still feels good.',j:'Last one at Canyon Gate.',m:'And the headlight.',h:'verify'}]},
{title:'Lights On',arc:'The Last Outpost is the end of the county road. Everyone Dad ever helped seems to be there tonight. So is Dad.',levels:[
{n:'Ofelia',r:'medic',t:'The night he towed me',s:'Ofelia, a nurse, once crashed on this road and was towed home by Dad. Tonight smoke drifted over the Outpost, and she’s waiting upwind.',k:'owes the tow driver her life',l:'He towed me home in 2015 and wouldn’t take a cent. I’ve waited eleven years to say thank you.',x:'He said: “I’ll know I did my job when my own kids come down this road.”',j:'We’re coming down it now.',m:'Hold on, Dad.',h:'smoke'},
{n:'Samson',r:'lineman',t:'Power for the Outpost',s:'Samson, a power lineman, is cut off by a fallen line he’s waiting for his crew to make safe.',k:'wired the tow driver’s porch lights',l:'I keep the lights on for a living. Tonight someone kept mine on.',x:'I wired every porch light at his camp. He wanted them bright enough to see from your garage.',j:'Bright enough for us to see.',m:'And we never looked out the window.',h:'cable'},
{n:'Gloria',r:'rancher',t:'Cattle in the dust',s:'Gloria, a rancher, pulled her truck off the road in a dust storm and waited it out, cattle safe behind her.',k:'lent the tow driver her land',l:'My cattle are safe. That’s all a rancher ever wants.',x:'He camps on my land. He said, “Just until they come home.” That was three years ago.',j:'Three years.',m:'Three years, waiting.',h:'dust'},
{n:'Matthias',r:'radio volunteer',t:'The last broadcast',s:'Matthias, a ham-radio operator, got a message to leave his post and go to a “company safe zone.” He confirmed with KDCR first and stayed.',k:'heard the tow driver’s last radio call',l:'Every voice on that radio was somebody’s someone. I kept listening.',x:'His last call tonight: “Kids, if you hear this, I left the light on. Come home.”',j:'He’s been calling us.',m:'And we finally heard.',h:'radio'},
{n:'Paz',r:'postmistress',t:'Letters never sent',s:'Paz, the Outpost postmistress, refused to hand over the mail-room code to company men. She locked the post office and walked out.',k:'keeps letters the tow driver never mailed',l:'Mail is private. Even letters nobody sent.',x:'He wrote you a letter every month for three years. Stamped. Addressed. Never mailed.',j:'Thirty-six letters.',m:'We’ll read every one.',h:'code'},
{n:'Iggy',r:'courier',t:'The final delivery',s:'Iggy, the clinic courier, packed the Outpost clinic’s sealed medicines before the evacuation. They need to reach the garage.',k:'delivered the tow driver’s medicine for years',l:'Last run of the night. Best run of my life.',x:'Every month he’d say: “Don’t tell my kids I’m sick.” I’m telling you now. He just needs rest.',j:'Dad’s sick?',m:'Then we take him home. Where he can rest.',h:'medicine',sup:1},
{n:'Coral',r:'student',t:'The water tank',s:'Coral, 10, stopped at the fence of the overflowing water tank and waved her flashlight instead of climbing in after her ball.',k:'got a gift from the tow driver tonight',l:'The ball can wait. That’s what the porch-light man said.',x:'He gave me two old keys and said: “If my kids come, give these to them.” Here.',j:'Our garage keys.',m:'The lock never changed.',h:'gate'},
{n:'Bruno',r:'welder',t:'The welded bridge',s:'Bruno, a welder, could have crossed the half-repaired bridge to the Outpost. He chose to wait for daylight.',k:'helped the tow driver build something',l:'I weld things that hold. Tonight I held still.',x:'We welded a new sign for the garage. He’s had it in his truck for a year, waiting to hang it.',j:'What does it say?',m:'We’ll see when we hang it. Together.',h:'crossing'},
{n:'Wen',r:'old friend',t:'Under the big porch light',s:'Wen, 79, is Dad’s oldest friend. He’s sitting under the biggest porch light at the Outpost, the landmark Dad told him to wait by.',k:'knows where Dad is right now',l:'He talks about you two every day. Every single day.',x:'He’s at the end of the road, by the truck. He saw your headlights coming. He’s scared you’re angry.',j:'I’m not angry. I just miss him.',m:'Me too. Let’s go get him.',h:'landmark'},
{n:'Dad',r:'tow truck driver',t:'The porch light',s:'Dad stayed at the Last Outpost with his truck’s lights on, guiding every last car out of the county. Now he’s the only one left on the road.',k:'kept the light on',l:'I kept the light on for everybody else’s kids. I just hoped mine would come.',j:'Dad.',m:'We’re taking you home.',h:'verify'}]}
]};
